@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+---
+id: dl-009-createpatch-recurses-into-objects-and-arrays-so-the-patch-describes-only-what-changed
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch recurses into objects and arrays so the patch describes only what changed"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Clients send changes instead of whole documents. A patch that replaces the root whenever anything differs
+defeats that purpose: a one-field change in a large document must yield a patch of one operation. RFC 6902
+allows `add`, `remove` and `replace` at any depth, so the patch can name exactly the changed places.
+
+## Decision
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` walks both documents together:
+
+- Equal values produce no operations.
+- Two objects: a member only in `from` gives a `remove`; a member only in `to` gives an `add`; a member in
+  both is compared recursively.
+- Two arrays: compared as described in dl-010.
+- Otherwise (different primitives, or an object or array against a value of another kind) the value is
+  replaced with a `replace` at that location. At the root this is a `replace` with path `""`.
+
+Paths escape `~` as `~0` and `/` as `~1` (RFC 6901). Values written into the patch are cloned, so the
+patch shares no structure with either input, as dl-004 requires of patch values. Inputs are read only.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A change in one place gives one operation at that place; an unchanged document gives an empty patch.
+- The patch is produced by reading both documents completely, so the cost grows with their size.
+- Differences in object member order are not reported, since JSON objects are unordered.
