@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+---
+id: dl-010-createpatch-strips-the-common-prefix-and-suffix-of-arrays-before-comparing-elements-by-index
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch strips the common prefix and suffix of arrays before comparing elements by index"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+An array in the patch is a change of positions: an insert or a delete shifts the later elements, so a
+naive index-by-index comparison would report every later element as replaced. An optimal diff of arrays
+(a longest common subsequence) gives the smallest patch but needs memory and time that grow with the
+product of the two lengths, which is too costly for large arrays in a library used by several services.
+
+## Decision
+
+For two arrays, `createPatch` first removes the common prefix and the common suffix. The middle parts are
+then compared by index: the elements at the same index are compared recursively (dl-009), and the extra
+elements of the longer part are removed or added at the index where the shorter part ends. Indices are
+absolute positions in the array as it is changed by the patch, so operations are emitted in ascending
+order and each one leaves the earlier positions correct.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A single inserted, deleted or changed element in an array gives one operation (or a small recursion into
+  that element), with no operations for the unchanged elements around it.
+- Several separate changes in the middle of one array may give more operations than an optimal diff would.
+  The result is still a correct patch, and its size is bounded by the lengths of the arrays.
+- The cost is linear in the lengths of the arrays, with no quadratic table.
